---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="The Credential Remembers Who. Nothing Remembers Why." description="Intent tokens: an identity layer for AI agents, built from standards we already trust.">
<style>
  :root {
    --bg:      #090908;
    --bg-2:    #0d0d0b;
    --bg-3:    #121210;
    --amber:   #c4972a;
    --amber-dim: rgba(196,151,42,0.12);
    --amber-border: rgba(196,151,42,0.15);
    --vessel:  #7eb8c4;
    --text:    #e8e6e1;
    --text-2:  #a09c94;
    --text-3:  #5a5752;
    --serif:   'DM Serif Display', Georgia, serif;
    --sans:    'DM Sans', system-ui, sans-serif;
    --mono:    'JetBrains Mono', ui-monospace, monospace;
    --max-w:   680px;
    --nav-h:   68px;
  }

  *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  html { background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased; }
  body { background: var(--bg); min-height: 100vh; }

  #nav {
    position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; z-index: 100;
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between;
    padding: 1.25rem 2rem;
    transition: background .25s, border-color .25s;
    border-bottom: 1px solid transparent;
  }
  #nav.scrolled {
    background: rgba(9,9,8,.92);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    border-color: rgba(196,151,42,0.15);
  }
  .nav-logo {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color .2s;
  }
  .nav-logo span { color: var(--amber); }
  .nav-logo:hover { color: var(--text-2); }
  .nav-back {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .65rem;
    color: var(--text-3);
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color .2s;
  }
  .nav-back:hover { color: var(--amber); }

  .article {
    max-width: var(--max-w);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 8rem 1.5rem 6rem;
  }

  .article-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .6rem;
    letter-spacing: .18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber);
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .article h1 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.15;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    letter-spacing: -.01em;
  }

  .article-meta {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .6rem;
    color: var(--text-3);
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
  }

  .article-subtitle {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .article p {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    margin-bottom: 1.6rem;
  }

  .article h2 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.55rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--text);
    margin: 3rem 0 1.25rem;
    letter-spacing: -.01em;
    line-height: 1.2;
  }

  .article strong {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .article em {
    color: var(--text);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .key-question {
    background: var(--amber-dim);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--amber);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
  }

  .key-question p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .attenuation-chain {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: .75rem;
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .8rem;
  }

  .chain-node {
    color: var(--text);
    background: var(--bg-3);
    border: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
    padding: .5rem .9rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .chain-arrow {
    color: var(--amber);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .chain-node.narrower {
    color: var(--text-2);
    font-size: .72rem;
  }

  .stack-table {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .stack-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 180px 1fr;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
  }

  .stack-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  .stack-label {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .6rem;
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--amber);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    background: var(--bg-2);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
  }

  .stack-desc {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: .875rem;
    color: var(--text-2);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .article-footer {
    margin-top: 4rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--amber-border);
  }

  .back-link {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .65rem;
    color: var(--text-3);
    letter-spacing: .1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color .2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover { color: var(--amber); }

  .author-note {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: .65rem;
    color: var(--text-3);
    letter-spacing: .06em;
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
</style>

<nav id="nav">
  <a class="nav-logo" href="/">matter<span>unknown</span></a>
  <a href="/writing" class="nav-back">~ Writing</a>
</nav>

<article class="article">
  <div class="article-label">Identity Security — July 2026</div>
  <h1>The Credential Remembers Who. Nothing Remembers Why.</h1>
  <div class="article-meta">matterunknown &nbsp;·&nbsp; July 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; 8 min read</div>
  <p class="article-subtitle">Intent tokens: an identity layer for AI agents, built from standards we already trust.</p>

  <p>There's a question I've started asking identity teams, and it reliably produces silence: when one of your AI agents takes an action — calls an API, touches a database, hands work to another agent — can you say what it was <em>authorized to do</em>? Not what credentials it held. What it was actually asked to do, by whom, and whether the action it took stayed inside that mandate.</p>

  <p>Nobody can answer it, because the identity stack was never built to hold the answer. We spent two decades perfecting identity for humans: authenticate the person, establish a session, gate the sensitive stuff behind MFA, log who did what. Then we spent the last few years bolting on non-human identity — discovering the service accounts, rotating the API keys, inventorying the OAuth grants. Watch the acquisition activity in that space and you can see the market deciding NHI is foundational.</p>

  <p>But agents are neither of those things. An agent is not a person with a session, and it's not a static workload with a predictable job. An agent is <em>delegated reasoning</em> — a task, handed off, that plans its own steps, calls its own tools, and increasingly spawns other agents to do parts of the work. And here is the failure mode that keeps me up at night: at every hop in that delegation chain, the original intent evaporates. The credential travels. The <em>reason</em> doesn't.</p>

  <h2>The gap between "who" and "why"</h2>

  <p>Look at what our existing primitives actually assert. A SPIFFE SVID tells you, with cryptographic confidence, which workload you're talking to. An OAuth access token tells you what scopes a client was granted — usually broad, usually static, usually decided long before this particular task existed. A service account tells you an application has standing permission to act.</p>

  <div class="key-question">
    <p>Every one of those answers "who." None of them answers "why." And with agents, <strong>why is the entire security question.</strong></p>
  </div>

  <p>An agent asked to summarize a contract and an agent asked to exfiltrate that contract can present identical credentials. The difference between them exists only in the intent — which today lives nowhere except a prompt log, if you're lucky.</p>

  <p>This is also why prompt injection is an identity problem wearing an application-security costume. When a manipulated agent does something it shouldn't, the credential check passes. The workload is legitimate. Every "who" question returns the right answer. The only thing that got corrupted was the intent, and we have no layer that carries or enforces it.</p>

  <h2>Intent tokens</h2>

  <p>The framework I've been building addresses this directly. An intent token is a short-lived, cryptographically signed token that binds three things together: the identity of the agent, the identity of the principal it acts for, and a machine-verifiable statement of the specific task it was delegated. Not "this agent may access the document service." Rather: "this agent, acting for this principal, was asked to summarize this document, and holds no authority beyond that."</p>

  <p>The property that makes this work across multi-agent systems is <strong>attenuation</strong>. When an agent delegates to another agent, it doesn't forward its token — it exchanges it for a new one whose intent is equal to or narrower than its own. Authority can only shrink as it moves down the chain.</p>

  <div class="attenuation-chain">
    <div class="chain-node">Research agent<br/><span class="narrower">"gather sources on topic X"</span></div>
    <span class="chain-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="chain-node">Fetch agent<br/><span class="narrower">"retrieve this URL"</span></div>
    <span class="chain-arrow">→</span>
    <div class="chain-node narrower">✕ cannot mint "send email" — never in the original mandate</div>
  </div>

  <p>The confused-deputy problem, which is really what most agent-security incidents amount to, becomes structurally difficult instead of procedurally discouraged.</p>

  <p>The other property is auditability. Because each token references the delegation that produced it, you get a verifiable chain from any individual action back to the original human intent. "Which agent did what, under whose authority, for what stated purpose" stops being a forensic reconstruction project and becomes a token lookup.</p>

  <h2>Deliberately boring plumbing</h2>

  <p>Here's the design decision I'd defend hardest: none of this is a new protocol. The delegation mechanics are OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange — RFC 8693, published in 2020, already supported in mature identity infrastructure. Workload identity comes from SPIFFE, which is how modern platforms already answer the "which workload is this" question. The tokens are JWTs signed with Ed25519. If you run an enterprise identity program, every load-bearing component here is something you have already evaluated, deployed, or at least argued about in an architecture review.</p>

  <div class="stack-table">
    <div class="stack-row">
      <div class="stack-label">Delegation</div>
      <div class="stack-desc">OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange — RFC 8693 (2020). Mature, already deployed across enterprise identity infrastructure.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-row">
      <div class="stack-label">Workload Identity</div>
      <div class="stack-desc">SPIFFE — answers "which workload is this," cryptographically, portably.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stack-row">
      <div class="stack-label">Token Format</div>
      <div class="stack-desc">Standard JWT, signed with Ed25519. No proprietary infrastructure, no new verifier.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>That's not a lack of ambition. It's the ambition. The agentic era is arriving faster than any new protocol could possibly achieve adoption, and identity teams do not need another standard to shepherd through a five-year maturity curve. They need the stack they already trust extended to answer one question it was never asked before. Intent tokens are that extension: RFC 8693's token exchange, with the exchanged token carrying a task assertion that attenuates on every hop.</p>

  <h2>Where I've been testing it</h2>

  <p>My lab for this is a multi-model pipeline I run on my own hardware: several models that generate code, cross-examine each other's output, run adversarial security review, and pass results through a sandbox before a human approves anything. It's exactly the kind of system that makes traditional identity thinking fall apart — multiple autonomous actors, real delegation, real tool access, no human in the loop between steps.</p>

  <p>Running intent tokens inside it has been clarifying. Every stage of the pipeline operates under a token scoped to its role, and the boundaries are enforced by verification, not by convention. It has also surfaced the hard edges — what intent granularity is actually practical, where attenuation logic gets subtle, what an agent should do when a task legitimately grows beyond its mandate. Those edges are the interesting research, and they're what the longer technical write-up will cover.</p>

  <h2>What's next</h2>

  <p>The framework is headed toward open source under matterunknown, with a fuller technical paper behind it. If you're working on agentic identity from any direction — building the platforms, running an identity program that's about to inherit a fleet of agents, or red-teaming these systems — I want to compare notes. Identity is the largest unmonitored attack surface, and agents are expanding it at machine speed. The layer that carries intent is where I think this gets won or lost.</p>

  <p class="author-note">Ryan Silver is the founder of matterunknown, an independent identity security research company.</p>

